%S A398003 0,0,2,9,64,540,3996,40229,374784,4345785,49972500,661462340,
%T A398003 8994996864,135664139780,2114330530536
%N A398003 Sum of excess shadings of all permutations of [n] using a special circular shading.
%C A398003 For a permutation sigma of [n], divide a circle into n equal sectors labeled 1,...,n.
%C A398003 For odd i, shade sigma(i) consecutive sectors starting from sector i in the counterclockwise direction.
%C A398003 For even i, do the same in the clockwise direction.
%C A398003 If c_i is the number of shadings of sector i, define the excess shadings by E(sigma) = Sum_i (c_i - min(c_1,...,c_n)).
%C A398003 The sequence gives the sum of E(sigma) over all permutations sigma in S_n.
%C A398003 For a permutation sigma, the excess shadings measures the total number of sector shadings above the minimum shading level of the circle.
%C A398003 The statistic E(sigma) measures the non-uniformity of the shading produced by the permutation. The value m is the maximum number of complete uniform layers that can be removed from all positions simultaneously. Therefore E(sigma) is exactly the amount of shading that remains after all common uniform coverage has been removed.
%C A398003 In particular, E(sigma)=0 if and only if all positions are shaded equally often. Larger values of E(sigma) correspond to greater imbalance in the coverage of the positions.
%C A398003 The sequence sums E(sigma) over all permutations in S_n in order to measure the aggregate non-uniformity of this special circular shading at size n. Thus a(n) is not associated with one particular permutation, but records the total excess shading generated by the entire permutation class S_n. This provides a global permutation statistic obtained from the special circular shading rule.
%F A398003 a(n) = Sum_{sigma in S_n} (n*(n+1)/2 - n*m(sigma)), where m(sigma) = min(c(1),...,c(n)) and c(i) is the number of times sector i is shaded.
%e A398003 For n = 3, consider the permutation (2,3,1).
%e A398003   Sector 1 is odd, so shade sectors 1,3.
%e A398003   Sector 2 is even, so shade sectors 2,3,1.
%e A398003   Sector 3 is odd, so shade sector 3.
%e A398003 Hence the numbers of shadings of the sectors are c = (2,1,3). So m = min(c) = 1 and E = (2-1) + (1-1) + (3-1) = 3.
%e A398003 For n = 3 there are 6 permutations. Their excess shadings are 0, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2. Therefore a(3) = 0+2+1+3+1+2 = 9.
%e A398003 For n = 4, consider the permutation (3,1,4,2).
%e A398003   Sector 1 is odd, so shade sectors 1,4,3.
%e A398003   Sector 2 is even, so shade sector 2.
%e A398003   Sector 3 is odd, so shade sectors 3,2,1,4.
%e A398003   Sector 4 is even, so shade sectors 4,1.
%e A398003 Hence c = (3,2,2,3). So m = min(c) = 2 and E = (3-2) + (2-2) + (2-2) + (3-2) = 2.
%e A398003 In general, the total number of shadings is 1+2+...+n = n*(n+1)/2, so E(sigma) = n*(n+1)/2 - n*m(sigma).
%o A398003 (Python)
%o A398003 from itertools import permutations
%o A398003 def excess_shadings(s):
%o A398003     n = len(s)
%o A398003     c = [0]*n
%o A398003     for i,x in enumerate(s):
%o A398003         for t in range(x):
%o A398003             c[(i+t)%n if i&1 else (i-t)%n] += 1
%o A398003     return n*(n+1)//2 - n*min(c)
%o A398003 def A398003(n): return 0 if n == 0 else sum(map(excess_shadings, permutations(range(1, n+1))))
%Y A398003 Cf. A000142, A002620, A077613, A145891, A306258.
